;==!========================================
;==! View/Modify JTAG chain

 'demo_board' MACH445  p 6 db.jdc / -s 1  -o Z -f db.out;

;==!========================================

;==! A JTAG chain file contains a list of JTAG parts and the operations
;==! to be performed on those parts. A part's position on the list
;==! determines its position in the JTAG chain.
;==!
;==! Ex: Your design has 3 JTAG parts connected into a JTAG chain.
;==!     The first part is an AMD MACH445, and its TDO is connected
;==!     to the next JTAG part which is a MACH465. In turn, its TDO is
;==!     connected to the TDI of the next JTAG part which is a non-AMD
;==!     part. The JTAG chain file should contain the following:
;==!
;==! 'ctrl_0'        MACH445  p 6 control.jed / -s 1 -s 2 -o Z -f control.out;
;==! 'state_machine' MACH465  p 6 state.jed   /           -o Z -f xfile.out;
;==! 'non_AMD_part'  part_XXX n 5;
;==!
;==! The format for each part description is:
;==!
;==! ['design_desc'] part_name opcode instr_width file_name [/ mfg options] ;
;==!
;==! where:
;==!     design_description = alphanumeric string (max 35 chars) beginning
;==!          with a letter enclosed in single quotes describing the design
;==!          (this is an optional field).
;==!     part_name = JTAG part name; e.g., MACH445
;==!     opcode    = instruction code; can be one of the following:
;==!               p - program part with JEDEC pattern in file_name and
;==!                   pattern verify
;==!               v - verify part against JEDEC pattern in file_name
;==!               r - read part contents and write to file_name
;==!               u - read the user code/signature bits
;==!               m - read the part JTAG ID code
;==!               n - put part into bypass mode
;==!     instr_width = number of bits in JTAG instruction register;
;==!          MACH445 and MACH465 have 6 bit instr registers; for
;==!          non-AMD JTAG parts, consult the part datasheet
;==!     file_name = JEDEC file for the specified operation
;==!
;==! Manufacturer options are optional. If you do specify them, put
;==! a '/' and then one of the following:
;==!
;==!   -s 1         = program security fuse 1
;==!   -s 2         = program security fuse 2
;==!   -o {0|1|X|Z} = set the IO pins to be either driving a 0, 1, or
;==!                  set the pins to tri-state mode
;==!   -f result_file = specify a filename to hold the results of the
;==!                  JTAG operation
;==!
;==! Terminate the part description with a semi-colon.
;==!
;==! Any non-AMD JTAG parts should be put into bypass mode (see above).

